## The runtime

This build runs on no engine, so the layer beneath the game is yours as well: the
frame loop and the delta time it hands each update, fitting the fixed logical stage
onto the canvas, the pointer and the keyboard the player drives the game with, the
audio, loading the produced asset files, and the on-screen debug overlay. The
specification in `specs/` states what Arc Foundry needs from that layer and fixes
nothing else about it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
## The {{engine.name}} engine

This build runs on the **{{engine.name}}** engine, installed as a dependency of the
project, so import it by its bare package name; there is nothing to add to
`package.json` for it and nothing to install.

The engine's own documentation is at `{{engine.docs}}`. It is the authoritative
description of the engine's API; the specification in `specs/` describes the game and
does not restate the engine. The documentation also carries a complete worked example
of unit-testing a game built on it.

Build the game on the engine rather than beside it. The engine owns the frame loop
and the delta time it hands your update, the input actions, the pointer, the audio
cues, asset loading, and the on-screen debug overlay. It also holds the game's state,
by value: each frame it hands `update` the current state read-only and keeps what
`update` returns as the next, so a frame builds the next state from the current one
rather than writing into it. Register what the game needs with the engine and use
what it gives back. What remains yours is the game itself: its simulation and
everything it draws.
{{else}}
## The {{engine.name}} engine

This build runs on the **{{engine.name}}** engine, installed as a dependency of the
project, so import it by its bare package name; there is nothing to add to
`package.json` for it and nothing to install.

The engine's own documentation is at `{{engine.docs}}`. It is the authoritative
description of the engine's API; the specification in `specs/` describes the game and
does not restate the engine.

Build the game inside the engine's framework rather than beside it. The engine owns
the frame loop and the delta time it hands each tick, rendering and the camera, the
input actions, the pointer, the audio cues, asset loading, and the on-screen debug
overlay, and the game is written as subclasses of its classes: a game instance, a
game mode, actors and their components, and the controllers that drive them. The
engine constructs, ticks, and renders them in a fixed order. Register what the game
needs with the engine and use what it gives back. What remains yours is the game
itself: its rules, its state, and everything it draws through the engine's pipeline.
{{/if}}

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Produce an implementation that matches the specification
exactly. `specs/overview.md` states what you implement, which files stay as they are,
the code quality the build is held to, and the commands that run over the finished
repository.

## The build interface

`npm ci` then `npm run build` must produce the static site, with no other manual
step, into `dist/` at the project root, with an `index.html` at the root of that
directory. That directory must run when served as-is from any base path, at the root
of a static file server or mounted under a sub-path of one. The scripts that do this
are already in `package.json`.

## The assets you produce

`specs/assets.md` is the authoritative contract for the art, animation, particle
effects, and sound this build ships. It names which tool produces each asset, the
exact path each file lands at under `assets/`, the bar each meets, and how each is
wired into the game.

The electrical effects are `particle-2d` systems played live through the particle
runtime already installed as a dependency of the project. Import it like any other
dependency.

## Driving the game in a browser

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The project's Playwright and its headless Chromium are installed, so you can serve
the built game and drive it in a real browser, walk a wave, check for console errors,
or exercise `window.__foundry`.
{{else}}
The project's Playwright and its headless Chromium are installed, so you can serve
the built game and drive it in a real browser, walk a wave, and check for console
errors.
{{/if}}

When you launch the browser, use the project's own Playwright: import it from your
project (for example `import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the
project-local CLI with `npx playwright`. There is no global Playwright on `PATH`; it
lives in your project's `node_modules` like any other dependency. Launch Chromium
with the `--no-sandbox` argument, since this container has no Chromium sandbox and a
default launch fails.

## The showcase

The game ships with its store-page presentation: a `showcase/` directory at the
project root holding a player-facing description of the game and a short carousel of
captured media. `specs/showcase.md` defines its contents — the description, the
`showcase.toml` carousel, and how the media are captured.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a git repository, initialized before you started with the
starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is published directly
from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is not published. When
the build is complete, commit everything: run `git add -A` then `git commit`, and
confirm `git status` reports a clean working tree with nothing uncommitted or
untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/`, the build output, and
coverage reports, so a clean tree means committing your source, your tests, your
`package-lock.json`, the produced files under `assets/`, your `showcase/` directory,
and your `README.md`.
